    "blurb": "Features a blistering 'The Other One' suite, soaring 'Sing Me Back Home', and solid 'Truckin'' and 'Cold Rain And Snow'. Jerry's guitar tone is pristine while Phil's bass drives the extended jams; recording quality varies but the soundboard capture is crisp.",
    "review": "The Strand Lyceum on May 24, 1972 finds the Dead in top form during their European spring trek. The first set kicks off with a bright 'Cold Rain And Snow' and rolls through classics before launching into a powerful 'Playing In The Band'. The second set erupts after a brief boogie‑flu opener, delivering a marathon that builds from tight funk on 'Truckin'' into an expansive drum duet and a monster 'The Other One' where Jerry and Bob trade lyrical leads. Phil's bass roars like a beast underneath the swirling improvisations, and his interplay with the drums keeps the momentum relentless. A highlight emerges in the heartfelt 'Sing Me Back Home', where Garcia’s solo is described as beautiful and perfectly voiced. The set closes with an exuberant 'Turn On Your Lovelight' segueing into 'The Stranger (Two Souls in Communion)', leaving the audience buzzing.",
